{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793006965,"nonce":"x5TEgz6O08fK8ucd3v9fONFFjO4=","title":"Restore non-Haxe syntax highlighting in browser editors","message":"Browser EditorPart currently routes only Haxe/RayScript and JSON to built-in highlighters; every other language is plain. Native TextMateSyntax explicitly returns null under WASM. The user again reported other-language files lacking highlighting.\n\nRoute supported built-in languages immediately, including module/JSX/TSX filename aliases. Share the existing Linguist/TextMate registry and token mapping in a small Rust syntax crate rather than loading the RayScript VM into the session. Add a bounded cancellable background syntax service to the native session. Requests must not block filesystem/process/document RPCs, require the normal session handshake, and release cancelled/closed work. Keep source/token/line/concurrency limits explicit and avoid retaining document source in the service.\n\nThe browser requests full-source lexical results through an ash-future worker after edits settle, accepts only the matching current source, and applies completed data at the UI frame boundary. Expose a reactive highlighter input in ashui so new token tables update an existing editor without losing selection/history/scroll. Release consumed response token tables once CodeDocument has packed them. Native editors retain the existing incremental TextMate session.\n\nVerify built-in routes, real Rust/TOML/YAML/shell/HTML grammar results, Unicode offsets, edits, cancellation/stale responses, nonblocking RPC, and native editor regressions. Rebuild WASM and the native session after this milestone, stage the verified assets and restart the existing endpoint for user testing.","files":null}]}